      "relatedLabel": "#132: test palindromes from endpoints and interior",
      "target": "http://127.0.0.1:18770/leetcode/132/#step1",
      "targetText": "02What to save, and whyHow can we avoid repeated work without missing a better partition? Dynamic programming stores answers to smaller questions for reuse. We save two kinds: P[l][r] tells whether the substring from zero-based index l through r, inclusive, is a palindrome. dp[k] is the fewest palindrome pieces covering the first k characters; those first characters form a prefix.Prefix states: see #115 for what each prefix-table cell means ↗First inspect the endpoints: aa has matching ends and an empty interior, so it is a palindrome. ab has different ends, so it is not. aab also has different ends. For a longer example, abba needs matching a endpoints AND a palindrome interior bb. abca has matching endpoints but a non-palindrome interior bc, so it fails. Lengths 1 and 2 need no inner cell; longer substrings reuse P[l+1][r−1].Now compare final pieces. The empty prefix has nothing to cover, so dp[0]=0 pieces. For a, final piece a gives dp[0]+1=1. For aa, final piece aa gives 1, while final piece a gives dp[1]+1=2; keep 1. For aab, aab and ab fail, leaving only final piece b: dp[2]+1=2. Thus dp=[0,1,1,2], and the final cut count is 2−1=1.To find the fewest pieces for the first r+1 characters, try each final-piece start l. Only a palindrome can extend the completed dp[l], adding one piece. Scan r from left to right: the interior is in an earlier column, and dp[l] is already complete. There is no circular dependency. Initialize the current minimum to n+1, above the maximum possible n pieces, then reduce it candidate by candidate.P[l][r] = (s[l] = s[r]) ∧ (r−l < 2 ∨ P[l+1][r−1])dp[0] = 0dp[r+1] = min { dp[l]+1 : 0 ≤ l ≤ r, P[l][r] = T }cuts = dp[n]−1"
