      "targetText": "032 · Choose digits step by stepWhat must change together after choosing a digit? Pick a boundary preset, keep n and k fixed, then choose the next digit. Inspect the equal blocks and cumulative ledger: the interval containing old r determines the digit. That digit moves from the unused list into the prefix, and r becomes the within-block remainder. Back restores all these quantities.In this experiment, m is the number of unused digits. The value r is the zero-based offset among all completions of the current prefix, always satisfying 0 ≤ r < m!. In every completed frame, prefix and unused together contain each digit from 1 to n exactly once.This is a JavaScript state simulation, not Python execution. Each frame completes one digit choice; the start has no choices and the end has n choices. No steps are omitted.Preset (changing restarts this experiment)Worked · n=4, k=9Block end · n=4, k=6Next block · n=4, k=7First item · n=3, k=1Last item · n=4, k=24Only item · n=1, k=1Custom inputOr choose valid inputs; applying restartsn k Apply n and kUndo last digitChoose next digitReset to startNumber of completed digit choicesInput n=4, k=9 · 0/4 digits chosenCurrent state: prefix (empty) · unused [1, 2, 3, 4] · r=8No digit chosen yet; r=k−1=8. The blocks below preview the first choice.Diagram: each box is a block of block=(m−1)! permutations. Intervals are inclusive zero-based offsets before the choice. q is an index in the unused list, not the digit itself. A ✓ marks the chosen block; the start does not mark an answer.Before this choice: m=4, 6 items per block, old r=8Digit 1 · index 0[0, 5]Digit 2 · index 1[6, 11]Digit 3 · index 2[12, 17]Digit 4 · index 3[18, 23]Cumulative choice ledger (completed operations)On narrow screens, scroll the ledger sideways. With a keyboard, focus the ledger and use left/right arrows to inspect the remaining columns.No completed choices yet.PositionBeforeDivisionAfterPython for this framer = k - 1"
